    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "to spin",
      "links": [],
      "level": 1
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "This page reviews a decidedly retinal type of kinetic\nart: the display of rotating objects whose speeds and/or patterns\nare designed to create optical artefacts that overrule ordinary,\n\"realistic\" perception. Rotating art pieces with less dazzling effects\nhave been catalogued under the keyword \"turn\".",
      "links": [
        {
          "text": "\"turn\"",
          "href": "/kinetics/turn",
          "external": false
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "spinning geissler-tubes",
      "links": [],
      "level": 3
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "In 1857, the German glass-blower and physicist Heinrich Geissler\ndeveloped the first practical high-voltage low-pressure gas discharge\ntube. Geissler could employ lower air pressures than before, obtained\nby means of a new mercury vacuum pump of his own design. And he\nmade good use of Ruhmkorff's new induction coil, to generate fast\nsequences of high-voltage pulses to drive the gas discharges.",
      "links": []
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Apart from its role in physical experimentation,\nthe \"Geissler-tube\" was mostly used for optical art. The\nartistic Geissler-tubes had a variety of fancy shapes; they employed\nuranium glass for green fluorescence, and powders and liquids for\nadditional color effects. The Geissler-tube also gave rise to an\nearly form of kinetic art, known as the Geissler-tube\nrotator: an electric motor which rotates a Geissler-tube\nat a fairly high speed. Since the Geissler-tube emits a periodic\nsequence flashes (rather than continuous light), looking at a\nmoving tube does not result in a motion blur, but in a superposition\nof discrete snapshots.",
      "links": []
    },
