    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Subjective Stroboscopy",
      "links": [],
      "level": 5
    },
    {
      "type": "figure",
      "src": "/images/kinetics/spin/img-VanRullenEtAl.2011-FIP-C.jpg",
      "alt": "",
      "caption": "Subjective direction reversal, as visualized by VanRullen et al. (2011)",
      "width": "399",
      "height": "199",
      "links": []
    },
    {
      "type": "figure",
      "src": "/images/kinetics/spin/img-Kline+Eagleman-2008-s.jpg",
      "alt": "",
      "caption": "A pattern employed by Kline et al. (2008)",
      "width": "200",
      "height": "200",
      "links": []
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "To a human observer, a continuously\nmoving visual pattern with a repetition frequency of ca. 13 Hz.\nmay appear to move in the direction opposite to the actual movement.\nThis phenomenon, discovered by J.F. Schouten (1967), is reminiscent\nof the \"wagonwheel effect\" that occurs when a movie-camera\nrecords rotating spokes whose repetition frequency approaches\nor exceeds the camera's sample frequency. To explain his observations,\nSchouten indeed postulated a periodicity in the behaviour of the\ncells in the retina.",
      "links": []
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "The subjective experience of looking at motion patterns\nin the critical frequency range is much more complex and interesting\nthan a simple direction reversal. The demonstrations of subjective\nstroboscopy in Schouten's lectures outperformed most optical and\nkinetic artworks.",
      "links": []
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "References",
      "links": []
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "J.F. Schouten: \"Subjective stroboscopy and a model of\nvisual movement detectors.\" In I. Wathen-Dunn (Ed.): Models\nfor the perception of speech and visual form. Cambridge, MA:\nMIT Press, 1967, pp. 44–45.",
      "links": []
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Keith A. Kline & David M. Eagleman: \"Evidence\nagainst the temporal subsampling account of illusory motion reversal.\" Journal\nof Vision, Vol. 8, no. 4 (April 18,\n2008), Article\n13.",
      "links": []
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Rufin VanRullen & Julien Dubois: \"The\nPsychophysics of Brain Rhythms.\" Frontiers in Psychology, Vol. 2 (August\n2011), Article 203.",
      "links": []
    },
